---
import { version } from "../../../../npm/magistr/package.json";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Workflow from "../components/Workflow.astro";
import ProductEvidence from "../components/ProductEvidence.astro";
const description = "Magistr keeps the roadmap, decisions and checked work for your project, so Codex or Claude Code can pick up where you left off.";
const jsonLd = [
  {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebSite","name":"Magistr","url":"https://magistr.dev",description},
  {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"Magistr","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"macOS (Apple Silicon)","softwareVersion":version,description,"url":"https://magistr.dev","downloadUrl":"https://magistr.dev/docs/installation","softwareRequirements":"macOS 15 or later, Rust 1.95 or later, repository access"}
];
---
<Base title="Build a project with Magistr" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="hero section-shell" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <h1 id="hero-title">Build a project with Magistr.</h1>
      <p class="hero-subtitle">Work with the coding agent you prefer.</p>
      <p class="hero-lede">Magistr keeps the plan and work history for your coding agent. Ask it to review the project, work through the changes, and check the result. When you start a fresh session, the next agent can read where you stopped.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a id="install-btn" class="button button-primary" href="#install">Install Magistr</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="#workflow">Watch a project take shape</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">Source preview for Apple Silicon macOS. Repository access required.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="workflow" class="section-shell section-block example-section" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="workflow-title">Watch an agent build a working support inbox.</h2>
        <p>Incoming mail, conversation threads, search, assignment, and the awkward cases: retries and two people changing the same request. Follow the actual build from its plan through the working app.</p>
      </div>
      <Workflow />
      <details id="evidence" class="example-sessions recorded-example">
        <summary>More agent sessions</summary>
        <p>These earlier captures show a separate Python fix in Codex, Claude Code and the desktop.</p>
        <ProductEvidence />
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="product" class="section-shell section-block product-section" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="product-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="product-title">Decide what needs doing, then work through it.</h2>
        <p>Start with the project you have. You can ask for a broad review, investigate one problem, or go straight to a fix.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-details">
        <article><h3>Review the project</h3><p>Have the agent read the code and compare it with the plan. Ask what's unfinished, what's getting in the way, and what it recommends changing. For an unfamiliar problem, ask it to research the options and keep the findings.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Choose the work</h3><p>Turn the review into roadmap items with a clear result and a way to check it. Put dependencies in order. Change the plan when you learn something; an old decision doesn't have to dictate the next change.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Let the agent work through it</h3><p>Finish one item, or give a longer run an area and a limit. Each finished item has its own checks and commit. A small fix can stay small; it doesn't need a new roadmap.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Try it and give feedback</h3><p>Read the diff, use the feature, and inspect the screens. Tell the agent what still feels wrong and have it revise the work. Ask for the checks that cover the change, and say when you want it pushed.</p></article>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link section-link" href="/docs/workflow">Example requests and commands</a>
    </section>

    <section id="install" class="section-shell section-block install-section" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="install-title">
      <div class="install-grid">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2 id="install-title">Install the source preview</h2>
          <p>v{version} is available to people with <a href="https://github.com/AlrikOlson/magistr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repository access<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>. Registry packages are not published yet.</p>
          <p>You'll need Apple Silicon macOS 15 or later, Rust 1.95 or later, Git, and the macOS command-line build tools. Bring your own Codex or Claude Code account.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/installation">Full installation instructions</a>
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel">
          <div class="code-label"><span>In your terminal</span><button class="copy-button" type="button" aria-label="Copy commands" data-copy="source-install" hidden>Copy commands</button></div>
          <pre id="source-install"><code>git clone https://github.com/AlrikOlson/magistr.git
cd magistr
cargo install --locked --path crates/magistr-cli
magistr --version</code></pre>
          <p class="copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="install-next">Then run <code>magistr init .</code> in your own project to connect your agent. Setup does not start implementation. Open a session to review the project and agree on a plan; use <code>magistr next</code> when you're ready to build.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
<script is:inline>
  document.querySelectorAll("[data-copy]").forEach((button) => {
    button.hidden = false;
    button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const source = document.getElementById(button.dataset.copy);
      const status = button.closest(".code-panel")?.querySelector(".copy-status");
      if (!source || !status) return;
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(source.textContent);
        status.textContent = "Commands copied.";
      } catch {
        status.textContent = "Copy is unavailable. Select the commands above to copy them.";
      }
    });
  });
</script>
